{
  "title": "Lumbrical Plus Finger",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/hand/6017/lumbrical-plus-finger",
  "subtitle": "Updated: May 19 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Lumbrical Plus Finger is characterized by paradoxical extension of the IP joints while attempting to flex the fingers**.**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made clinically with extension of the IP joints of one digit with attempted flexion of all fingers (making a fist).",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is observation if patient is minimally symptomatic. Operative tenodesis of FDP to terminal tendon or lumbrical release is indicated if symptoms affect patient's activity demands.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most common in **middle** finger (2nd lumbrical)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "FDP 3, 4, 5 share a common muscle belly",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "cannot independently flex 2 digits without pulling on the third",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "index finger has independent FDP belly",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "when making a fist following FDP2 transection, it is possible to only contract FDS2 (and not FDP2), thus avoiding paradoxical extension",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**FDP disruption distal to the origin of the lumbicals** (most common)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "can be due to",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "**FDP transection**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "**FDP avulsion**",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [
                            {
                              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6017/images/conditions%20causing%20lumbrical%20plus.jpg",
                              "caption": ""
                            }
                          ],
                          "importance": "5",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**DIP amputation**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**amputation through middle phalanx shaft**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6017/images/amputation%20.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**\"too long\" tendon graft**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6017/images/lp.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "lumbricals originate from FDP",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "with FDP laceration, FDP contraction leads to pull on lumbricals",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "lumbricals pull on lateral bands leading to PIP and DIP extension of involved digit",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "with the middle finger, when the FDP is cut distally, the FDP shifts ulnarly (because of the pull of the 3rd lumbrical origin)(bipennate)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "this leads to tightening of the middle finger lumbrical (2nd lumbrical, unipennate), and amplifies the \"lumbrical plus\" effect",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Lumbricals",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6017/images/lumbricals.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "1st and 2nd lumbricals",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**unipennate**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**median nerve**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "originate from radial side of FDP2 and FDP3 respectively",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "3rd and 4th lumbricals",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**bipennate**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**ulnar nerve**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "3rd lumbrical originates from FDP 3 & 4",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "4th lumbrical originates from FDP 4 & 5",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "all insert on **radial** side of extensor expansion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "History",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "recent volar digital laceration (FDP transection) or sudden axial traction on flexed digit (FDP avulsion)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "notices that when attempting to grip an object or form a fist, 1 digit sticks out or gets caught on clothes",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6017/images/lumbrical%20plus.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "paradoxical IP extension with grip (fingers extend while holding a beer can)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "tenodesis of FDP to terminal tendon or reinsertion to distal phalanx",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "FDP lacerations",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "do NOT suture flexor-extensor mechanisms over bone",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "lumbrical release",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "if FDP is retracted or segmental loss makes it impossible to fix",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "NOT done in the acute setting as it does not occur consistently enough to warrant routine lumbrical sectioning acutely",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "contraindications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "do not transect lumbricals 1 & 2 if there is concomitant ulnar nerve palsy",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "with ulnar nerve paralysis, the interosseous muscles are also lost",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "(interosseus muscles extend the IP joints)",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**transect at base of flexor sheath (in the palm)**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
